infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Choosing a CLI library: stdlib flag vs cobra
  desc The decision line is surface area, not ergonomics — start with flag, migrate when subcommands hurt
  compares
    - label stdlib flag
      children
        - label Zero dependencies
          desc Nothing added to audit forever; the static binary stays lean
        - label One verb, single-dash flags
          desc -verbose not --verbose; hand-roll subcommands with flag.NewFlagSet
        - label Typed flags, free -h, exit-2
          desc Generates -h and exits 2 on bad input out of the box
        - label Start here
          desc One verb plus a handful of flags — a framework would be a function call
    - label cobra / urfave-cli
      children
        - label Pulls pflag and a dep tree
          desc A real cost you now audit forever, in a binary meant to avoid sprawl
        - label Nested git-style subcommands
          desc Twelve verbs stay manageable where a switch on os.Args[1] turns miserable
        - label Generated completions and man pages
          desc Shell completions for three shells, man pages automated
        - label Migrate when the surface grows
          desc Pay the dependency cost knowingly once the subcommand switch hurts
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
